Idiopathic Muscle Cramps
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Intense painful involuntary muscle contractions, Predominantly nocturnal calf involvement, Relief with passive stretching, Normal basic metabolic panel

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-026
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, and unrelenting bilateral calf spasms causing absolute inability to bear weight and intense distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient suffering from intense, excruciating nocturnal muscle spasms in the lower limbs, resulting in an inability to walk and severe pain. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out acute deep vein thrombosis or acute arterial ischemia, and to implement immediate mechanical and pharmacological relief of the spasms prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up screaming because both my calves knotted up completely solid; the pain is blinding, and I can't even stand up."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 2-hour episode of sudden, intense, and exquisitely painful involuntary muscle contractions predominantly involving the bilateral calf muscles. The spasms awakened the patient from a deep sleep (nocturnal predilection). The patient describes visible, hard knots in the muscles during the acute spasms. The intense cramping has only partially subsided, leaving residual severe muscle soreness and a fear of moving the legs. The patient completely denies objective persistent muscle weakness, recent trauma, or severe wasting.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, refusing to mobilize out of fear of triggering another severe spasm.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls if mobilizing during an acute spasm; extreme sleep disruption.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/88 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, lying supine, guarding both lower limbs.
Physical Exam: Bilateral gastrocnemius muscles are tense and tender to palpation, but without localized erythema, swelling, or extreme heat. Prompt relief of the acute severe cramping is achieved upon forceful passive dorsiflexion of the feet. Peripheral pulses (dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial) are bounding and completely intact, ruling out vascular compromise. Complete absence of prominent neurological signs such as spasticity, clonus, or sensory loss. Documented history of entirely normal basic metabolic panel, normal serum magnesium and calcium, and normal thyroid function.
Environmental Observations: Heavy, restrictive blankets at the foot of the bed, enforcing prolonged plantar flexion during sleep.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; appropriately hydrated based on history.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Idiopathic Muscle Cramps - Status: Acute nocturnal exacerbation
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out by the bilateral presentation, intact peripheral pulses, lack of pitting edema, and the immediate relief achieved with simple passive stretching.

Acute Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out due to completely normal, bounding distal pulses, warm extremities, and absence of pallor.

Hypocalcemia/Electrolyte Derangement - Unlikely given the strictly nocturnal calf presentation without carpopedal spasms, and historically normal metabolic panels.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to severe, excruciating bilateral leg pain causing functional collapse and mimicking an acute vascular emergency (such as acute ischemia or deep vein thrombosis). The severity of the pain and the patient's absolute inability to weight-bear necessitated an immediate, hands-on clinical examination. By definitively confirming intact peripheral pulses and normal neurological function, the physician safely excluded a life-threatening vascular event. The prompt application of targeted physical stretching and pharmacological muscle relaxants immediately stabilized the patient, preventing an inappropriate emergency department transfer, satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed physician-guided passive stretching on-site with excellent clinical effect. Prescribed a short course of oral Diazepam 2mg strictly nocte for 3 days to break the severe spasm cycle.
Positive Behavioral Change: Immediately upon waking with a cramp, forcefully pull the toes upward toward the shin and hold for 30 seconds. This physical action engages the antagonist muscle (tibialis anterior), reflexively shutting down the spastic gastrocnemius (reciprocal inhibition), rewarding the patient with instant, self-directed pain relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week if nocturnal cramping persists, to consider quinine trial or further electrolyte screening.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing unilateral, severe, continuous leg swelling, loss of sensation or coldness in the feet, or chest pain and shortness of breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This diagnosis assumes previously normal metabolic panels remain stable; if spasms spread to other muscle groups, reassessment is needed.
Clinical Course: Idiopathic nocturnal cramps are intensely painful but mechanically completely benign. The residual soreness today is from the severe muscle contraction and will fade like post-workout soreness.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if one leg suddenly becomes persistently swollen, hot, and red, or if your foot turns cold and pale.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your calf muscle is forcefully contracting and refusing to let go, similar to a severe charley horse. The nighttime medication will temporarily relax the threshold of the muscle, but mechanically stretching the muscle is the definitive cure.
Actionable Steps: Loosen the blankets at the foot of your bed tonight so your feet aren't forced to point downward while you sleep. Sleeping with the feet in a neutral position prevents the calf from remaining in a shortened, cramp-prone state all night, directly lowering the chance of another attack.

